<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-ftc">
  <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

  <section xml:id="sec-area-function">
    <title>The Area Function</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be a continuous function. Define a new function by
    </p>

    <me>
      A(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt.
    </me>

    <p>
      The value <m>A(x)</m> represents the net area accumulated under
      <m>f</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To understand how <m>A</m> changes, compare <m>A(x+h)</m> and
      <m>A(x)</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      A(x+h)-A(x)
      =
      \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt.
    </me>

    <p>
      If <m>h</m> is small, the area over this short interval is
      approximately the area of a rectangle of height <m>f(x)</m>
      and width <m>h</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      A(x+h)-A(x)\approx f(x)h.
    </me>

    <p>
      Dividing by <m>h</m> gives
    </p>

    <me>
      \frac{A(x+h)-A(x)}{h}\approx f(x).
    </me>

    <p>
      Taking the limit as <m>h\to 0</m> leads to the Fundamental
      Theorem of Calculus.
    </p>

  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ftc-part-one">
    <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part I</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-ftc-one">
      <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part I</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> is continuous and
        </p>

        <me>
          A(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt,
        </me>

        <p>
          then
        </p>

        <me>
          A'(x)=f(x).
        </me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      In words, differentiating an accumulated integral returns the
      original function.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-ftc-direct">
      <title>Differentiating an integral</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Differentiate
        </p>

        <me>
          F(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt.
        </me>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus,
        </p>

        <me>
          F'(x)=f(x).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-leibniz-rule">
    <title>Variable Limits and the Leibniz Rule</title>

    <p>
      If the upper limit of integration is a function of <m>x</m>,
      we combine the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus with the chain rule.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-ftc-chain-rule">
      <title>A variable upper limit</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Differentiate
        </p>

        <me>
          F(x)=\int_a^{g(x)} f(t)\,dt.
        </me>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus gives the value of the
          integrand at the upper limit, and the chain rule contributes
          the derivative of the upper limit:
        </p>

        <me>
          F'(x)=f(g(x))g'(x).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      If the lower limit depends on <m>x</m>, reversing the limits
      introduces a minus sign.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-ftc-lower-limit">
      <title>A variable lower limit</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Differentiate
        </p>

        <me>
          F(x)=\int_{g(x)}^b f(t)\,dt.
        </me>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Reverse the order of integration:
        </p>

        <me>
          F(x)
          =
          -\int_b^{g(x)}f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          F'(x)=-f(g(x))g'(x).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-leibniz-variable-limits">
      <title>Variable upper and lower limits</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          If
        </p>

        <me>
          F(x)=\int_{u(x)}^{v(x)}f(t)\,dt,
        </me>

        <p>
          then
        </p>

        <me>
          F'(x)
          =
          f(v(x))v'(x)
          -
          f(u(x))u'(x).
        </me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

  </section>

</chapter>